  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder than normal through Monday with gradually\ndiminishing winds and clouds.\n\nNorthwest flow off the Great Lakes continues. This is resulting\nin intermittent snow showers along and west of the Allegheny\nFront, with sprinkles possible to the east through this evening\nas multiple waves aloft pass through.\n\nOtherwise, it will be partly to mostly cloudy for most as\nstratocumulus pivots overhead. Northwest gusts of 15 to 30 mph\nwill gradually subside through Monday. Gusts will be slower to \nsubside at higher elevations (especially at night compared to \nthe lower elevations). Clouds will also gradually thin and \nscatter out during this period.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions persist through the end of the week \nas temperatures gradually increase.\n\nBroad upper-level ridging building over the central Plains will \ngradually track east throughout the week, with surface high pressure \nlingering over the Mid-Atlantic for several days in the wake of last \nweek's cold front. Winds remain light for the foreseeable future as \nthis ridging builds overhead before transitioning to a zonal flow \npattern potentially by the upcoming weekend. Overall, this will lead \nto a week of predominantly clear skies and increasing temperatures \nto well above seasonable normals, potentially nearing 70F as we \napproach mid-to-late week. Overnight lows remain in the 40s mid to \nlate week.\n\nLooking towards next weekend, precipitation chances could increase \nsomewhat as low pressure forming over the Great Lakes tracks east \ntowards the coast.",
